use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence
from ../../../behaviors/Selection import Selection
from ../../../behaviors/Traversal import Traversal

export ui-component Tabs:
    description:
        Tabs split related content into panels that share one space, with a
        row of tabs to switch between them, like "Overview | Reviews |
        Specifications". Exactly one panel shows at a time.

    behaviors:
        - @{Selection}
        - @{Traversal}
        - @{Presence}

    value:
        Exactly one tab is selected, and its panel is the one showing. Clicking
        the selected tab again does nothing. The component decides which tab
        starts selected, can change it itself, and is told whenever the user
        changes it.

    content:
        Each tab shows a label, an icon, or both. A tab with only an icon still
        has a name that assistive tools can read out. Each panel is named by
        its tab, and assistive tools know which tab controls which panel.

    keyboard:
        Tab moves into the row of tabs in one step, landing on the selected
        tab. The arrow keys along the row move between tabs, wrapping around
        at the ends, and Home and End go to the first and last. Pressing Tab
        again moves into the showing panel: to the first thing in it that can
        take focus, or to the panel itself if it holds nothing that can.

    activation:
        The component decides whether moving to a tab with the arrow keys also
        selects it, or only moves focus, leaving the user to press Enter or
        Space to select it. Selecting as focus moves suits panels that show
        instantly. Waiting for a key press suits panels that are slow to show,
        such as ones that load from a server.

    orientation:
        Tabs usually run across, above their panels, and then Left and Right
        move between them, reversed for right-to-left languages. They can also
        run down the side, and then Up and Down move between them.

    disabled:
        A disabled tab is shown but can't be selected, and the arrow keys skip
        over it.

    hiddenPanels:
        Panels that aren't showing are hidden from everyone, including
        assistive tools. Whether they stay rendered while hidden, keeping
        things like half-typed text, is covered by Presence and decided by the
        component.

    addresses:
        The component can tie each tab to an address, so that reloading or
        sharing the page keeps the same tab selected. How is up to the chosen
        library.

    overflow:
        Whether tabs that don't fit in the row scroll, wrap, or move into a
        "more" list is up to the chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for picking a value. That's a SegmentedControl
        - Not for moving between pages of a site. That's a NavigationMenu
        - No closing, adding, or dragging tabs to reorder them, as in a web
            browser
